    \u0440\u0443\u043a\u0430 \u043e\u0431 \u0440\u0443\u043a\u0443, \u0438 \u043a\u0430\u043a
    \u0441\u0438\u043b\u0430 \u0432\u043e\u043b\u0438 \u0438 \u043f\u043e\u0434\u0434\u0435\u0440\u0436\u043a\u0430
    \u0431\u043b\u0438\u0437\u043a\u0438\u0445 \u043f\u043e\u0437\u0432\u043e\u043b\u044f\u044e\u0442
    \u043e\u0434\u0435\u0440\u0436\u0430\u0442\u044c \u043f\u043e\u0431\u0435\u0434\u0443 \u043d\u0430\u0434,
    \u043a\u0430\u0437\u0430\u043b\u043e\u0441\u044c \u0431\u044b, \u043d\u0435\u043f\u0440\u0435\u043e\u0434\u043e\u043b\u0438\u043c\u044b\u043c\u0438
    \u043e\u0431\u0441\u0442\u043e\u044f\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0430\u043c\u0438."
  interestingFactsEn: "John Cesh's life, even beyond his scientific breakthroughs, is full of amazing, and sometimes henish, stories, and they paint the image of a man whose genius mind has always been on its unpredictable path, especially during his illness, and these are some of the most interesting facts.\n\nThe genius and eccentricity of his youth His unusualness has been since his early years. The teacher's recommendation: Perhaps the most famous part of his life story is a letter of recommendation for Princeton's postgraduate from his professor at Carnegie Institute of Technology, R. J. Daffin, which contained only one sentence: \"This man is a genius.\"\"The Games of Mind\" in reality: The famous scene in a movie where Cesh explains the balance in a bar is actually made up. In reality, he came to his theory by studying more abstract mathematical concepts, not by watching the strategies of dating. The inventor of the game: Princeton invented a very popular strategic board game on a board with hexagonal cells.She became a hit in the student dorm, and everyone called her \"Cash\" or \"John.\" Later, Parker Brothers released her as \"Hex.\" Talking to Einstein: As a graduate student, Cesh, full of insolence, asked Albert Einstein for an audience at the Institute of Advanced Studies, he gave him his ideas on physics, but after an hour of conversation, the great scientist advised him to learn physics better.\n\nThe Ghost and the weirdness of the disease The periods of his severe illness, which lasted for decades, are encircled by almost mystical and tragic stories.The party in the diaper: At the 1958 New Year's Ball, he appeared in the same diaper and ribbon, with a baby bottle of milk sitting on his wife's lap, Alicia's weird inscriptions: Princeton's students called him \"The Phantom of Fein Hall.\" He wandered around at a math school building, often barefoot and in disproportionate clothes, and left mysterious inscriptions on the boards.One of them said, \"The Bar Mitzvah Mao Zedong was 13 years, 13 months and 13 days later than the circumcision of Brezhnev.\" Life: In a lecture, he announced to his amazing colleagues that he was on the cover of Life magazine as Pope John XXIII.\n\n♪ Later recognition and daily chickens even after returning to normal life Cesh remained an eccentric figure. The shortest Nobel work: His thesis, for which he received the Nobel Prize, contained only 27 pages with a \"very generous\" double inter-structural interval, as his colleague joked. The first article, \"Cesh's balance\", was published in 1950 and occupied only one page (317 words).The thought of the Nobel Prize: When Cesh received the Nobel Prize, his first reaction to the monetary portion (it was about $310,000) was a dry joke that the sum was not so large. \"The perfect money\": In recent years he was deeply interested in the idea of creating an indexed \"ideal currency\" whose value would have been constant, like a meter or a kilo.This idea was unorthodox, but no one was in a hurry to call it insane. These chickens are not just funny stories, but an attempt to look into the mind of a man who took the world quite differently from most of us. His case is a unique indication of how genius and madness can go hand in hand, and how the power of will and support of the loved ones can defeat seemingly insurmountable circumstances."
